packages: .

-- Pin the solver's VIEW of the Hackage index (#1354). Without this the
-- resolved build plan is a function of WHEN a build ran rather than of
-- what this repository contains: outside `base >=4.20 && <5` not one
-- build-depends entry carries a version bound, so any upload to Hackage
-- can move a dependency with no commit here. That is what lets a green
-- master turn red from an unrelated diff, stops a bisect from
-- reproducing a historical build, and quietly invalidates any
-- measurement taken across two worktrees built a week apart.
--
-- Pinning the view lets the solver run normally, so a .cabal edit still
-- resolves without regenerating anything. That is why there is
-- deliberately no cabal.project.freeze: a freeze file pins exact
-- versions and flags and must be regenerated on every dependency
-- change.
--
-- This timestamp must stay AT OR BEFORE the index snapshot baked into
-- the CI image. .github/ci/Dockerfile runs `cabal update` while the
-- image is built, and the image tag is the hash of that file plus
-- .github/workflows/ci-image.yml, so the baked snapshot only advances
-- when one of those two files changes (#784). A pin NEWER than the
-- baked snapshot would make every CI run depend on its per-run `cabal
-- update` succeeding — the exact coupling this pin exists to remove.
-- ci.yml's "Resolve dependency plan" step therefore resolves ONCE
-- before that update, so a pin that outruns the image fails the build
-- instead of hiding behind the refresh.
--
-- Locally, your own index must COVER this timestamp; the pin bounds
-- what the solver may see, it cannot complete an index that stops
-- earlier. An older index makes cabal stop and tell you to run `cabal
-- update`, which is the whole remedy and is expected rather than
-- something to engineer around.
--
-- To bump it: `cabal update`, then set the timestamp cabal reports (or
-- any earlier one) and re-resolve — `cabal build all --dry-run` writes
-- dist-newstyle/cache/plan.json, whose package/version set is what to
-- compare before and after. Moving the pin PAST the image's baked
-- snapshot means editing .github/ci/Dockerfile (or ci-image.yml) in the
-- same change so a new image, carrying a newer baked index, is built
-- and published for it; without that the pre-update resolve above
-- fails, which is the point.
index-state: 2026-08-14T00:00:00Z

-- Parallel builds (#443, reworked for #471). The library is one
-- 360+-module package, so package-level `jobs` alone can't parallelize
-- it; the per-package `ghc-options: -j` below makes each ghc --make
-- session compile modules in parallel up to $ncpus.
jobs: $ncpus

-- Do NOT re-enable `semaphore: True` (cabal's -jsem jobserver): the
-- cabal 3.16 jsem protocol is unsound under concurrent cabal
-- invocations — the normal multi-worktree workflow here. Semaphore
-- names derive from process CPU time (near-deterministic, so every
-- invocation contends on the same few names), the named kernel objects
-- outlive killed builds, and a client blocked on an unlinked-and-
-- recreated name parks forever with zero CPU: multi-hour silent build
-- hangs (#471). Upstream redesigned the protocol (v2, unreleased);
-- revisit only when a released cabal/GHC pair speaks v2. Plain `-j`
-- gives the same module-level parallelism with no cross-process state;
-- concurrent worktree builds may oversubscribe cores, which degrades
-- gracefully instead of deadlocking.
semaphore: False

package synarchy
  ghc-options: -j
